// Classic string enum (emits a runtime object)
enum LogLevel { Info = "info", Warn = "warn", Error = "error" }
function logEnum(level: LogLevel, msg: string) { console.log(`[${level}] ${msg}`); }
logEnum(LogLevel.Warn, "disk low");
// @ts-expect-error -- plain strings aren't accepted for a string enum
logEnum("warn", "disk low");

// Alternative: const object + derived union type
const Level = { Info: "info", Warn: "warn", Error: "error" } as const;
type Level = (typeof Level)[keyof typeof Level];   // "info" | "warn" | "error"

function log(level: Level, msg: string) { console.log(`[${level}] ${msg}`); }
log(Level.Error, "boom");
log("info", "plain strings work too");

const allLevels = Object.values(Level);              // iterate at runtime
console.log(allLevels);                              // ["info", "warn", "error"]

concepts.body:
Built-in narrowing works inside one function. To turn a check into a reusable function, write a **type predicate**: a function returning `boolean` whose return type is `param is Type`.

```typescript
function isString(x: unknown): x is string {
  return typeof x === "string";
}
```

When the function returns `true`, the caller's variable is narrowed to `string`; when `false`, to whatever remains. This is how you validate unknown data and how you filter arrays: `items.filter(isDefined)` returns `T[]` instead of `(T | undefined)[]`.

The compiler trusts your predicate — it does not verify that the body really checks what the signature claims. A predicate that returns `true` too easily is a lie the checker will happily believe, so keep them small and test them.

Since TypeScript 5.5 the compiler can **infer** a predicate for simple functions. `values.filter(v => v !== undefined)` now yields the non-undefined element type without any annotation, as long as the arrow's body is a check the compiler can follow both ways.

An **assertion function** throws instead of returning a boolean. Its return type is `asserts param is Type` (or `asserts condition`):

```typescript
function assertIsDefined<T>(v: T): asserts v is NonNullable<T> {
  if (v == null) throw new Error("Expected a value");
}
```

(`<T>` lets the function work for any type — see Generics — and `NonNullable<T>` means `T` without `null` and `undefined`.) After a call, the rest of the scope sees the narrowed type. Assertion functions must be declared with an explicit type annotation when called through a variable (e.g. as a `const` arrow), otherwise the compiler reports an error asking for one.

Predicates can also be methods (`this is Admin`), which is handy for class hierarchies.

