concepts.body:
TypeScript lets several declarations with the same name combine into one entity — **declaration merging**.

**Interfaces merge**: declaring `interface Box { width: number }` twice with different members gives one interface with all of them. Non-function members must not conflict; methods with the same name become overloads.

A **namespace** is an older TypeScript way to group names under one object (`namespace format { ... }`). **Namespaces merge with functions, classes and enums**, which lets you attach static-like members to a function or class — the trick behind many older JavaScript libraries' types (`function $()` that also has `$.ajax`).

The practical payoff is **module augmentation**: adding to types declared by another module. In a file that is itself a module (it has an `import` or `export`), write:

```typescript
import "express";
declare module "express-serve-static-core" {
  interface Request { user?: { id: string } }
}
```

You augment the module that actually *declares* the interface (here Express's internal types package), and you can only add to existing declarations — not add new top-level exports.

**Global augmentation** works the same way for global types, using `declare global { ... }` inside a module:

```typescript
declare global {
  interface Window { analytics: Analytics }
}
export {};
```

The `export {}` matters: in a file without imports or exports, `declare global` is not allowed, and a plain `interface Window` in such a script file merges globally anyway — confusing.

Augmentations live in `.ts` or `.d.ts` files that the project includes. Keep them in one obvious place (e.g. `src/types/`), because they change types everywhere and are easy to forget. Augmenting built-ins like `Array.prototype` is possible but pollutes every consumer of your code; libraries should avoid it.

concepts.code:
// Interface merging
interface Box { width: number }
interface Box { height: number }
const box: Box = { width: 2, height: 3 };
// Function + namespace merging: a callable with properties
function format(value: number): string {
  return value.toFixed(format.precision);
}
namespace format {
  export let precision = 2;
}
format.precision = 1;
// Global augmentation (this file is a module because of the export below)
declare global {
  interface Array<T> {
    last(): T | undefined;
  }
}
Object.defineProperty(Array.prototype, "last", {
  value: function <T>(this: T[]) { return this[this.length - 1]; },
});

console.log(box.width * box.height, format(3.14159), [1, 2, 3].last());
export {};

interface Animal { name: string }
interface Dog extends Animal { bark(): void }
interface Cat extends Animal { meow(): void }
const dog: Dog = { name: "Rex", bark() {} };

// Covariance: producers of Dog are producers of Animal
const makeDog: () => Dog = () => dog;
const makeAnimal: () => Animal = makeDog;          // ok
// Contravariance: consumers of Animal can consume Dog
const logAnimal = (a: Animal) => console.log(a.name);
const onDog: (d: Dog) => void = logAnimal;         // ok
const dogOnly = (d: Dog) => d.bark();
// @ts-expect-error -- a Dog-only handler can't accept every Animal
const onAnimal: (a: Animal) => void = dogOnly;
// Method syntax is bivariant (unsound, but allowed)
interface Handlers { handle(a: Animal): void }
const h: Handlers = { handle: dogOnly };           // compiles!
// Arrays are covariant (also unsound)
const dogs: Dog[] = [dog];
const animals: Animal[] = dogs;
animals.push({ name: "Tom", meow() {} } as Cat);   // dogs now contains a cat

interface Producer<out T> { make(): T }
const p: Producer<Animal> = { make: makeDog };
onDog(dog); makeAnimal(); h.handle(dog); p.make(); void onAnimal;

